// Practice quiz: Mutability and aliasing
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/python-fundamentals/mutability-and-aliasing

::o1 shared dict::config \= dict(debug\=False); settings \= config; settings["debug"] \= True. What is config["debug"]? {
=True #Correct. settings \= config makes a second name for the same dict, so the change shows through both names.
~False #Not quite. Assignment does not copy; there is only one dict.
~KeyError #Not quite. The key exists in the one shared dict.
~None #Not quite. The value was set to True through the other name.
}

::o1 passing a list::def reset(xs)\: xs.clear(). After scores \= [7, 9] and reset(scores), what is scores? {
=[] #Correct. The parameter xs is another name for the caller's list, so clear() empties that list.
~[7, 9] #Not quite. The function received the same list object, not a copy.
~None #Not quite. clear() returns None, but scores still names the now-empty list.
~An error, because xs is local #Not quite. The name xs is local; the object it points at is shared.
}

::o1 rebinding a parameter::def replace(xs)\: xs \= [0]. After nums \= [1, 2] and replace(nums), what is nums? {
=[1, 2] #Correct. xs \= [0] only moves the local name xs to a new list; the caller's list is untouched.
~[0] #Not quite. Assignment inside the function rebinds the local name; it does not change the caller's object.
~[1, 2, 0] #Not quite. Nothing is appended.
~[] #Not quite. Nothing clears the caller's list.
}

::o1 identity check::a \= [1]; b \= [1]; c \= a. Which is True? {
=a is c #Correct. c \= a makes c point at the same object as a.
~a is b #Not quite. a and b are two separate lists that happen to be equal.
~b is c #Not quite. c shares a's list, not b's.
~None of them #Not quite. a is c is True, because c \= a copied the reference.
}

::o2 string method::word \= "hi"; word.upper(); print(word). What prints? {
=hi #Correct. Strings are immutable; upper() returns a new string, which this code throws away.
~HI #Not quite. upper() does not change word; you would need word \= word.upper().
~None #Not quite. word still names the original string.
~An error #Not quite. Calling upper() and ignoring the result is legal.
}

::o2 tuple::Which statement about t \= (1, 2) is true? {
=t[0] \= 5 raises TypeError, because tuples are immutable #Correct. Tuples cannot change in place; you build a new tuple instead.
~t.append(3) adds an item #Not quite. Tuples have no append method.
~t +\= (3,) changes the original tuple object #Not quite. +\= makes a new tuple and moves t to it.
~Tuples are mutable lists with round brackets #Not quite. Lists are mutable; tuples are not.
}

::o3 mutable default::def log(msg, history\=[])\: history.append(msg); return history. What does the second call log("b") return after log("a")? {
=['a', 'b'] #Correct. The default list is created once, when def runs, so every call shares it.
~['b'] #Not quite. That would need a new list per call, which this default does not give.
~['a'] #Not quite. "b" is appended to the shared list too.
~None #Not quite. The function returns the list.
}

::o3 shallow copy::orig \= [[1], [2]]; dup \= orig[\:]; dup[0].append(5). What is orig? {
=[[1, 5], [2]] #Correct. A slice is a shallow copy\: the outer list is new, but the inner lists are shared.
~[[1], [2]] #Not quite. Only copy.deepcopy would also copy the inner lists.
~[[1], [2], 5] #Not quite. The 5 went into the first inner list.
~[[5], [2]] #Not quite. append adds after the 1; it does not replace it.
}
